# App navigation

The frame around a signed-in app: where you are, where you can go, and how to
get back. Compose it from [Header](squonk-ui://components/header),
[Sidebar](squonk-ui://components/sidebar), [NavList](squonk-ui://components/nav-list)
for the links between pages, [Breadcrumb](squonk-ui://components/breadcrumb),
[Link](squonk-ui://components/link) and, on phones, a
[Drawer](squonk-ui://components/drawer) for the menu.

**Example:** Named navigation with the current page marked, and a breadcrumb that ends at the page's own heading.

```tsx
export function AppFrameExample() {
  const [current, setCurrent] = React.useState("Sightings");
  return (
    <div style={{ display: "grid", gridTemplateColumns: "minmax(0, 10rem) minmax(0, 1fr)", gap: 16, minWidth: 0 }}>
      <nav aria-label="Account">
        <VStack gap="xxs">
          {NAV.map((item) => (
            <Link
              key={item}
              href={`#${item.toLowerCase()}`}
              variant={item === current ? "default" : "subtle"}
              weight={item === current ? "bold" : "regular"}
              underline="never"
              aria-current={item === current ? "page" : undefined}
              onClick={(event: React.MouseEvent) => {
                event.preventDefault();
                setCurrent(item);
              }}
            >
              {item}
            </Link>
          ))}
        </VStack>
      </nav>
      <VStack gap="sm">
        <Breadcrumb items={[{ label: "Home", href: "#home" }, { label: current }]} homeIcon />
        <Heading level={3} as="h2">{current}</Heading>
        <Text tone="secondary">The page's main content. Its heading matches the current navigation item.</Text>
      </VStack>
    </div>
  );
}

/** Tabs switch views of one thing; they are not site navigation. */
```

## The rules

- **Name each navigation landmark** (`<nav aria-label="Account">`) so screen-reader
  users can tell two apart.
- **Mark the current page** with `aria-current="page"` and a visible style, and
  make the page's heading match the item.
- **Keep the reading order sensible** when the sidebar stacks above the content
  on a phone, and add a skip link when the navigation is long.
- **Your app owns routing.** Pass your router's link component to Breadcrumb's
  `linkAs`; Sidebar is a layout region, not a router or a modal.

## Tabs switch views; links change pages

Use [Tabs](squonk-ui://components/tabs) to switch between views of one
thing on the same page. Use links for places in the app.

**Example:** Views of one sighting. The URL and the page heading don't change.

```tsx
export function TabsForViewsExample() {
  return (
    <Tabs
      listLabel="Sighting views"
      items={[
        { value: "summary", label: "Summary", content: <Text>Tear volume: 340 mL</Text> },
        { value: "lines", label: "Details", content: <Text>Tear trail of 40 m; two pools mapped.</Text> },
        { value: "history", label: "History", content: <Text>Logged 28 September.</Text> },
      ]}
    />
  );
}

/** A menu used for page navigation. */
```

## Menus are for actions

**Do:** Navigation as links in a named nav: each one is a real link people can open in a new tab.

```tsx
export function AppFrameExample() {
  const [current, setCurrent] = React.useState("Sightings");
  return (
    <div style={{ display: "grid", gridTemplateColumns: "minmax(0, 10rem) minmax(0, 1fr)", gap: 16, minWidth: 0 }}>
      <nav aria-label="Account">
        <VStack gap="xxs">
          {NAV.map((item) => (
            <Link
              key={item}
              href={`#${item.toLowerCase()}`}
              variant={item === current ? "default" : "subtle"}
              weight={item === current ? "bold" : "regular"}
              underline="never"
              aria-current={item === current ? "page" : undefined}
              onClick={(event: React.MouseEvent) => {
                event.preventDefault();
                setCurrent(item);
              }}
            >
              {item}
            </Link>
          ))}
        </VStack>
      </nav>
      <VStack gap="sm">
        <Breadcrumb items={[{ label: "Home", href: "#home" }, { label: current }]} homeIcon />
        <Heading level={3} as="h2">{current}</Heading>
        <Text tone="secondary">The page's main content. Its heading matches the current navigation item.</Text>
      </VStack>
    </div>
  );
}

/** Tabs switch views of one thing; they are not site navigation. */
```

**Don't:** Navigation in a dropdown menu: menus promise app-like keyboard behaviour and hide where people can go.

```tsx
export function MenuAsNavigationExample() {
  return (
    <DropdownMenu
      trigger={<Button as="span" variant="secondary">Go to…</Button>}
      items={[
        { key: "overview", label: "Overview" },
        { key: "sightings", label: "Sightings" },
        { key: "payments", label: "Payments" },
      ]}
    />
  );
}

// ---- Menus and commands -----------------------------------------------------

/** A named menu of commands on a row, with the destructive one last. */
```

The [Member dashboard] shows
the full frame: header, sidebar, a drawer menu on phones and a breadcrumb. For
the layout around the navigation, and how it changes on phones, see
[Page layouts and app shells](squonk-ui://guides/patterns-page-layouts-and-app-shells).

# Cards and landing sections

A card is a surface that groups one thing. Whether the whole card is clickable
depends on how many things people can do with it.

## A card that goes somewhere

Use [InteractiveCard](squonk-ui://components/interactive-card) when the whole
card has one destination or one action: `as="a"` with an `href` to navigate, or
`onClick` for an action. Its heading names it. A plain
[Card](squonk-ui://components/card) is just a surface.

**Do:** One destination, so the whole card is the link.

```tsx
export function LinkCardExample() {
  return (
    <InteractiveCard as="a" href="#sightings" variant="outlined" style={{ maxWidth: 360 }}>
      <VStack gap="xs">
        <Heading level={4} as="h3">Sighting reports</Heading>
        <Text tone="secondary">Log, correct and see what has been verified.</Text>
      </VStack>
    </InteractiveCard>
  );
}

/** A card with several actions: a plain Card with real buttons. */
```

**Do:** Two actions, so a plain Card holds two real buttons. Never nest buttons or links inside an interactive card.

```tsx
export function CardWithActionsExample() {
  return (
    <Card variant="outlined" style={{ maxWidth: 360 }}>
      <VStack gap="sm">
        <Heading level={4} as="h3">Annual dues, 2026–27 season</Heading>
        <Text tone="secondary">Due 28 November.</Text>
        <Cluster gap="sm">
          <Button variant="primary">Pay dues</Button>
          <Button variant="secondary">View last statement</Button>
        </Cluster>
      </VStack>
    </Card>
  );
}

const PLANS = [
  { name: "Tadpole", price: "$0", detail: "For members under 18", features: ["Night watches", "Newsletter"] },
  { name: "Keeper", price: "$29", detail: "For households", features: ["Up to 6 members", "Field-trip priority", "Phone support"], featured: true },
  { name: "Life member", price: "Talk to us", detail: "For lifelong keepers", features: ["One payment", "Archive access"] },
];

/** Comparable plans: same order of information in every card, one featured. */
```

Keep the focus ring visible and test long headings. A pointer cursor or hover
lift doesn't make something interactive; the element underneath must be a link
or a button.

## Comparing plans

Compose [Section](squonk-ui://components/section),
[SectionHeading](squonk-ui://components/section-heading),
[Grid](squonk-ui://components/grid) and Cards. Put the same
information in the same order in every card, feature one option at most, and
compare with words, not colour alone. The cards stack in reading order on a phone.

**Example:** One plan featured, with a badge that says why. Every plan lists price, audience and features in the same order.

```tsx
export function PricingExample() {
  return (
    <Grid columns={{ mobile: 1, tablet: 3 }} gap="md">
      {PLANS.map((plan) => (
        <Card key={plan.name} variant={plan.featured ? "featured" : "outlined"}>
          <VStack gap="sm">
            <Cluster gap="xs" align="center">
              <Heading level={4} as="h3">{plan.name}</Heading>
              {plan.featured ? <Badge tone="primary">Most popular</Badge> : null}
            </Cluster>
            <Text weight="bold">{plan.price}{plan.price.startsWith("$") ? " a month" : ""}</Text>
            <Text tone="secondary">{plan.detail}</Text>
            <ul style={{ margin: 0, paddingInlineStart: "1.2em" }}>
              {plan.features.map((feature) => <li key={feature}><Text as="span">{feature}</Text></li>)}
            </ul>
            <Button variant={plan.featured ? "primary" : "secondary"}>Choose {plan.name}</Button>
          </VStack>
        </Card>
      ))}
    </Grid>
  );
}

// ---- QR handoff -------------------------------------------------------------

/** A code with its purpose stated and a way in for people who can't scan. */
```

Your app owns prices, billing periods and the purchase flow; when the
billing period changes, every figure must change with it.

# Destructive actions

**Example:** Deleting a draft happens at once, with undo offered in a toast that stays. Cancelling the membership can't be undone, so it asks first; while it works, the dialog can't be dismissed.

```tsx
export function DestructiveExample() {
  return (
    <ToastProvider position="bottom-right">
      <DestructiveInner />
    </ToastProvider>
  );
}

function DestructiveInner() {
  const toast = useToast();
  const [drafts, setDrafts] = React.useState(["Draft sighting, 12 Sep", "Draft sighting, 3 Sep"]);
  const [confirming, setConfirming] = React.useState(false);
  const [closing, setClosing] = React.useState(false);
  const [closed, setClosed] = React.useState(false);
  return (
    <VStack gap="md">
      <VStack gap="xs">
        {drafts.map((draft) => (
          <Cluster key={draft} gap="sm" align="center">
            <Text>{draft}</Text>
            <Button
              variant="ghost"
              onClick={() => {
                setDrafts((d) => d.filter((x) => x !== draft));
                toast.show({ tone: "info", message: `Deleted "${draft}". Undo from Recently deleted.`, duration: 0 });
              }}
            >
              Delete draft
            </Button>
          </Cluster>
        ))}
        {drafts.length === 0 ? <Text tone="secondary">No drafts. <Link href="#restore" onClick={(e: React.MouseEvent) => { e.preventDefault(); setDrafts(["Draft sighting, 12 Sep", "Draft sighting, 3 Sep"]); }}>Restore them</Link></Text> : null}
      </VStack>
      <div>
        <Button variant="danger" onClick={() => setConfirming(true)} disabled={closed}>{closed ? "Membership cancelled" : "Cancel membership"}</Button>
      </div>
      <ConfirmModal
        open={confirming}
        title="Cancel your membership?"
        message="You won't be able to log sightings or join night watches. This can't be undone; to rejoin you'll need a new application."
        confirmLabel="Cancel membership"
        cancelLabel="Keep membership"
        danger
        loading={closing}
        onCancel={() => setConfirming(false)}
        onConfirm={() => {
          setClosing(true);
          window.setTimeout(() => {
            setClosing(false);
            setConfirming(false);
            setClosed(true);
            toast.success("Your membership is cancelled.");
          }, 900);
        }}
      />
    </VStack>
  );
}

/** A confirmation whose answer is "Yes". */
```

## Prefer undo when you can

**Our guidance** (the sources don't compare the two): if an action can be reversed,
do it straight away and offer **Undo**. A confirmation for every routine delete
trains people to click through without reading. Offer undo where the user is
looking — next to the item, or in a toast that stays until dismissed
(`duration: 0`) — and also from somewhere durable, such as a "Recently deleted"
view, because a toast can be missed.

## Confirm what can't be undone

When the action is permanent, or affects other people, ask first with
[ConfirmModal](squonk-ui://components/modal):

- **Title** names the action and its object: "Delete project 'Atlas'?"
- **Message** states the consequence, including that it can't be undone.
- **Confirm button** says what it does ("Delete project"), uses `danger`, and is
  never "Yes" or "OK".
- **Cancel** stays available until the work starts. While it runs, `loading`
  blocks dismissal so the user can't leave a half-finished change.

Put destructive commands last in a [DropdownMenu](squonk-ui://components/dropdown-menu)
with `tone: "danger"`, separated from the rest.

**Do:** The title names the action, the message states the consequence, and the button says what it does. Try “Cancel membership” above.

```tsx
export function DestructiveExample() {
  return (
    <ToastProvider position="bottom-right">
      <DestructiveInner />
    </ToastProvider>
  );
}

function DestructiveInner() {
  const toast = useToast();
  const [drafts, setDrafts] = React.useState(["Draft sighting, 12 Sep", "Draft sighting, 3 Sep"]);
  const [confirming, setConfirming] = React.useState(false);
  const [closing, setClosing] = React.useState(false);
  const [closed, setClosed] = React.useState(false);
  return (
    <VStack gap="md">
      <VStack gap="xs">
        {drafts.map((draft) => (
          <Cluster key={draft} gap="sm" align="center">
            <Text>{draft}</Text>
            <Button
              variant="ghost"
              onClick={() => {
                setDrafts((d) => d.filter((x) => x !== draft));
                toast.show({ tone: "info", message: `Deleted "${draft}". Undo from Recently deleted.`, duration: 0 });
              }}
            >
              Delete draft
            </Button>
          </Cluster>
        ))}
        {drafts.length === 0 ? <Text tone="secondary">No drafts. <Link href="#restore" onClick={(e: React.MouseEvent) => { e.preventDefault(); setDrafts(["Draft sighting, 12 Sep", "Draft sighting, 3 Sep"]); }}>Restore them</Link></Text> : null}
      </VStack>
      <div>
        <Button variant="danger" onClick={() => setConfirming(true)} disabled={closed}>{closed ? "Membership cancelled" : "Cancel membership"}</Button>
      </div>
      <ConfirmModal
        open={confirming}
        title="Cancel your membership?"
        message="You won't be able to log sightings or join night watches. This can't be undone; to rejoin you'll need a new application."
        confirmLabel="Cancel membership"
        cancelLabel="Keep membership"
        danger
        loading={closing}
        onCancel={() => setConfirming(false)}
        onConfirm={() => {
          setClosing(true);
          window.setTimeout(() => {
            setClosing(false);
            setConfirming(false);
            setClosed(true);
            toast.success("Your membership is cancelled.");
          }, 900);
        }}
      />
    </VStack>
  );
}

/** A confirmation whose answer is "Yes". */
```

**Don't:** “Are you sure?” and “Yes” make people click through without reading.

```tsx
export function VagueConfirmExample() {
  const [open, setOpen] = React.useState(false);
  return (
    <>
      <div><Button variant="secondary" onClick={() => setOpen(true)}>Delete</Button></div>
      <ConfirmModal
        open={open}
        title="Are you sure?"
        message="Do you want to continue?"
        confirmLabel="Yes"
        cancelLabel="No"
        onCancel={() => setOpen(false)}
        onConfirm={() => setOpen(false)}
      />
    </>
  );
}

// ---- Focus management ---------------------------------------------------------

/** Deleting the focused row moves focus to the next one, not the top of the page. */
```

Your app owns `open`, the operation and what happens if it fails: clear
`loading` and say what went wrong, so people aren't left stuck in the dialog.
Focus starts on the least destructive choice and returns to the trigger when the
dialog closes. Don't use a dialog for routine news; that's a toast.

## Afterwards

Say what happened, and move focus somewhere useful if the deleted item had it:
the next row, or the list. See [Focus management](squonk-ui://guides/patterns-focus-management).

