# Focus management

Keyboard and screen-reader users follow focus. Move it only when the user's
context changes, and always to somewhere they expect.

| When… | Focus goes to | Handled by |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A dialog opens | The dialog itself (so its title is read first) | [Modal](squonk-ui://components/modal) |
| A dialog closes | Back to the control that opened it, or the next sensible place if that control has gone | Modal |
| A menu or list opens | Into it, with the first item highlighted | [DropdownMenu](squonk-ui://components/dropdown-menu), [Select](squonk-ui://components/select) |
| A menu or list closes | Back to its trigger | DropdownMenu, Select |
| A form submit fails | The error summary or the first invalid field, once | Your form |
| Content loads or refreshes | Nowhere — announce it instead | Your page |
| A focused row is deleted or filtered out | The nearest remaining row | [Table](squonk-ui://components/table) in grid mode; your list otherwise |
| A toast or alert appears | Nowhere | [Toast](squonk-ui://components/toast), [Alert](squonk-ui://components/alert) |

## Don't strand focus

When the focused element disappears (a deleted row, a closed panel), browsers
send focus to the top of the page. Move it to the next item, or to the list
that held it, before removing the element.

**Example:** Tab to a Remove button and press Enter: focus moves to the next member's button, or to the list when the last one goes.

```tsx
export function FocusAfterDeleteExample() {
  const [items, setItems] = React.useState(["Tui Anderson", "Moana Reid", "Rimu Parata", "Hemi Walker"]);
  const refs = React.useRef(new Map<string, HTMLButtonElement | null>());
  const listRef = React.useRef<HTMLUListElement>(null);
  const remove = (index: number) => {
    const next = items[index + 1] ?? items[index - 1];
    setItems((list) => list.filter((_, i) => i !== index));
    requestAnimationFrame(() => (next ? refs.current.get(next)?.focus() : listRef.current?.focus()));
  };
  return (
    <ul ref={listRef} tabIndex={-1} aria-label="Members" style={{ margin: 0, padding: 0, listStyle: "none", display: "grid", gap: 8 }}>
      {items.map((item, i) => (
        <li key={item}>
          <Cluster gap="sm" align="center">
            <Text style={{ minWidth: 140 }}>{item}</Text>
            <Button variant="ghost" ref={(el: HTMLButtonElement | null) => { refs.current.set(item, el); }} onClick={() => remove(i)}>
              Remove {item}
            </Button>
          </Cluster>
        </li>
      ))}
      {items.length === 0 ? <li><Text tone="secondary">No members left.</Text></li> : null}
    </ul>
  );
}

// ---- Toasts, alerts and dialogs -----------------------------------------------

/** Each message on the surface that fits it. */
```

## Don't steal focus on load

Don't move focus when a page opens or data arrives, unless almost everyone needs
the same single control straight away (a sign-in form, for example).

## Keep focus visible

All library components show a focus ring for keyboard use and none for mouse
clicks. Don't remove it. In grids and menus the focused or highlighted item shows
it.

