# Conditional and repeating fields

## Questions that depend on an answer

Show follow-up questions with `form.Subscribe`, which re-renders only when the
value it selects changes. Validate them with `superRefine`, only when they
apply, so a hidden field can never block the submit.

Put follow-ups straight after the question that reveals them, so they are next
in the reading and tab order.

```tsx
import React from "react";
import { z } from "zod";
import { Alert } from "@squonkstack/ui";
import { useAppForm } from "@squonkstack/ui/form";

// Questions that only apply to some answers are validated only when they
// apply, so a hidden field can never block the submit.
const schema = z
  .object({
    leads: z.string().min(1, "Say whether you lead night watches"),
    groupSize: z.string(),
    nextWatch: z.string(),
    watchFrequency: z.string(),
  })
  .superRefine((v, ctx) => {
    if (v.leads !== "yes") return;
    if (!/^[1-9]\d*$/.test(v.groupSize)) ctx.addIssue({ code: "custom", path: ["groupSize"], message: "Enter how many people join your watches, as a number" });
    if (!v.nextWatch) ctx.addIssue({ code: "custom", path: ["nextWatch"], message: "Enter the date of your next watch" });
    if (!v.watchFrequency) ctx.addIssue({ code: "custom", path: ["watchFrequency"], message: "Choose how often you run watches" });
  });

/** Follow-up questions that appear, and validate, only when they apply. */
export function WatchLeaderDetailsForm() {
  const [result, setResult] = React.useState<string>();
  const form = useAppForm({
    defaultValues: { leads: "", groupSize: "", nextWatch: "", watchFrequency: "" },
    validators: { onChange: schema },
    onSubmit: async ({ value }) =>
      setResult(value.leads === "yes" ? `Registered as a watch leader for ${value.groupSize}.` : "No watch-leader registration needed."),
  });

  return (
    <form.AppForm>
      <form.Form aria-label="Night-watch leader details">
        <form.ErrorSummary />
        <form.AppField name="leads">
          {(field) => (
            <field.RadioGroup
              legend="Do you lead night watches?"
              direction="row"
              options={[{ value: "yes", label: "Yes" }, { value: "no", label: "No" }]}
            />
          )}
        </form.AppField>
        <form.Subscribe selector={(state) => state.values.leads === "yes"}>
          {(leads) =>
            leads ? (
              <>
                <form.AppField name="groupSize">{(field) => <field.TextField label="How many people join your watches?" inputMode="numeric" />}</form.AppField>
                <form.AppField name="nextWatch">{(field) => <field.TextField label="Date of your next watch" type="date" />}</form.AppField>
                <form.AppField name="watchFrequency">
                  {(field) => (
                    <field.Select
                      label="How often do you run watches?"
                      placeholder="Choose one"
                      options={[
                        { value: "weekly", label: "Weekly" },
                        { value: "fortnightly", label: "Fortnightly" },
                        { value: "monthly", label: "Monthly" },
                      ]}
                    />
                  )}
                </form.AppField>
              </>
            ) : null
          }
        </form.Subscribe>
        <div><form.SubmitButton>Continue</form.SubmitButton></div>
        {result ? <Alert tone="success" role="status">{result}</Alert> : null}
      </form.Form>
    </form.AppForm>
  );
}
```

## Lists people add to

Give the array field `mode="array"` and use `pushValue` and `removeValue`. Each
row's fields are named by index (`members[0].name`), so their errors and the
summary links work like any other field.

- Group each row in a `fieldset` with a numbered legend, so "Full name" is heard
  as "Household member 2, Full name".
- Name remove buttons for their row ("Remove household member 2"), never just "Remove".
- Validate the list itself as well as its rows, for "Add at least one household member".

```tsx
import React from "react";
import { z } from "zod";
import { Alert, Button, Heading } from "@squonkstack/ui";
import { useAppForm } from "@squonkstack/ui/form";
import { AddIcon, DeleteIcon } from "@squonkstack/ui/icon";
import styles from "./examples.module.css";

const householdMember = z.object({
  name: z.string().trim().min(1, "Enter the household member's full name"),
  email: z.email("Enter the household member's email address in the right format"),
});
const schema = z.object({ members: z.array(householdMember).min(1, "Add at least one household member") });
type HouseholdMember = z.infer<typeof householdMember>;

const blank = (): HouseholdMember => ({ name: "", email: "" });

/**
 * A repeating group: add and remove the people in a Keeper (household)
 * membership. Each row's fields are named by index (`members[0].name`), so errors and summary links find them.
 */
export function HouseholdMembersForm() {
  const [saved, setSaved] = React.useState<string>();
  const form = useAppForm({
    defaultValues: { members: [blank()] },
    validators: { onChange: schema },
    onSubmit: async ({ value }) => setSaved(`Saved ${value.members.length} household member${value.members.length === 1 ? "" : "s"}.`),
  });

  return (
    <form.AppForm>
      <form.Form aria-label="Household members">
        <form.ErrorSummary />
        <form.AppField name="members" mode="array">
          {(list) => (
            <>
              {list.state.value.map((_, i) => (
                <fieldset key={i} className={styles.group}>
                  <legend className={styles.groupLegend}>
                    <Heading level={3}>Household member {i + 1}</Heading>
                  </legend>
                  <form.AppField name={`members[${i}].name`}>{(field) => <field.TextField label="Full name" />}</form.AppField>
                  <form.AppField name={`members[${i}].email`}>{(field) => <field.TextField label="Email" type="email" />}</form.AppField>
                  {list.state.value.length > 1 ? (
                    <div>
                      <Button variant="ghost" onClick={() => list.removeValue(i)}>
                        <DeleteIcon size="control" /> Remove household member {i + 1}
                      </Button>
                    </div>
                  ) : null}
                </fieldset>
              ))}
              <div>
                <Button variant="secondary" onClick={() => list.pushValue(blank())}>
                  <AddIcon size="control" /> Add another household member
                </Button>
              </div>
            </>
          )}
        </form.AppField>
        <div><form.SubmitButton>Save household members</form.SubmitButton></div>
        {saved ? <Alert tone="success" role="status">{saved}</Alert> : null}
      </form.Form>
    </form.AppForm>
  );
}
```

# Custom fields

For money, use the library's `field.CurrencyField`
([CurrencyField](squonk-ui://components/currency-field)); this page uses a
whole-dollar amount only to show the technique.

When a form needs a control the library doesn't ship, build it from React Aria
and bind it with `useFieldContext`, inside `form.AppField`:

- **React Aria brings the behaviour.** Here, `NumberField` formats the amount for
  the locale, accepts arrow keys, and wires the label, description and error.
- **The form brings the state.** `useFieldContext` gives the value,
  `handleChange`, `handleBlur` and the errors. Show the error after blur or a
  submit attempt, like the library's fields do.
- **The theme brings the look.** Style with the `--squonk-*` tokens, using the same
  control height, border, radius and focus ring as the other fields.

```tsx
import React from "react";
import { FieldError, Group, Input, Label, NumberField, Text } from "react-aria-components";
import { z } from "zod";
import { Alert } from "@squonkstack/ui";
import { useAppForm, useFieldContext } from "@squonkstack/ui/form";
import { useStore } from "@tanstack/react-form";
import styles from "./examples.module.css";

/**
 * How a custom field is built, from React Aria's NumberField and
 * bound with `useFieldContext`. React Aria brings the behaviour (locale
 * formatting, arrow keys, the label, description and error wiring); the form
 * brings the value, blur state and error; the theme tokens bring the look.
 * For real money inputs use the library's CurrencyField (`field.CurrencyField`).
 */
function WholeDollarField({ label, description }: { label: string; description?: string }) {
  const field = useFieldContext<number | null>();
  const attempts = useStore(field.form.store, (state) => state.submissionAttempts);
  const shown = field.state.meta.isBlurred || attempts > 0;
  const error = shown ? field.state.meta.errors.map((e) => (typeof e === "string" ? e : e?.message)).find(Boolean) : undefined;

  return (
    <NumberField
      className={styles.numberField}
      name={field.name}
      value={field.state.value ?? NaN}
      onChange={(value) => field.handleChange(Number.isNaN(value) ? null : value)}
      onBlur={field.handleBlur}
      isInvalid={Boolean(error)}
      validationBehavior="aria"
      formatOptions={{ style: "currency", currency: "NZD", maximumFractionDigits: 0 }}
      minValue={0}
    >
      <Label className={styles.numberLabel}>{label}</Label>
      <Group className={styles.numberGroup}>
        <Input className={styles.numberInput} />
      </Group>
      {description && !error ? <Text slot="description" className={styles.numberHint}>{description}</Text> : null}
      <FieldError className={styles.numberError}>{error}</FieldError>
    </NumberField>
  );
}

const schema = z.object({
  gift: z.number({ error: "Enter your planned gift" }).min(1, "Enter your planned gift"),
});

export function PlannedGiftForm() {
  const [saved, setSaved] = React.useState<string>();
  const form = useAppForm({
    defaultValues: { gift: null as number | null },
    validators: { onChange: schema },
    onSubmit: async ({ value }) =>
      setSaved(`${(value.gift ?? 0) >= 1000 ? "You'll be listed as" : "You won't be listed as"} a Swamp Fund patron.`),
  });

  return (
    <form.AppForm>
      <form.Form aria-label="Planned gift">
        <form.ErrorSummary />
        <form.AppField name="gift">
          {() => <WholeDollarField label="Planned Swamp Fund gift in the next 12 months" description="Gifts of $1,000 or more are listed. A rough figure is fine." />}
        </form.AppField>
        <div><form.SubmitButton>Check</form.SubmitButton></div>
        {saved ? <Alert tone="info" role="status">{saved}</Alert> : null}
      </form.Form>
    </form.AppForm>
  );
}
```

If you need the field in more than one product, it belongs in the library: ask
for it rather than copying it between apps. To add your own field to `form.AppField`
everywhere in an app, use `extendForm`.

# Multi-step forms

Split a long form when the steps are separate topics, or when a later step
depends on an earlier answer. Keep a single page when people need to see
everything together.

- **One form per step**, each with its own schema. A step can't be left until it
  is valid, and its error summary covers only that step.
- **Keep the answers in the parent**, so Back returns to filled-in fields.
- **Say where they are**: "Step 2 of 3", and the step's name as its heading.
- **Move focus to the new heading** on each step, so screen-reader users hear
  where they have arrived.
- **End with a check page** and send nothing until it is confirmed.

## One long page, or steps

| The form… | Use |
| --- | --- |
| Is short, or people need to see every answer together | One page |
| Is long but people fill it in one sitting, in any order | One page with a heading (or a `Fieldset`) per topic, and the error summary at the top |
| Has separate topics, or later questions depend on earlier answers | Steps, as here |
| Is a quick task started from another page | A [Modal](squonk-ui://components/modal) or [Drawer](squonk-ui://components/drawer), only if it's a few fields; otherwise a page |

Save long forms as people go, or warn before they leave with unsaved answers.
The [Join the Society]
example is a long single-page form with sections and a review drawer.

```tsx
import React from "react";
import { z } from "zod";
import { Alert, Button, Heading, ProgressBar, Text } from "@squonkstack/ui";
import { useAppForm } from "@squonkstack/ui/form";
import styles from "./examples.module.css";

const details = z.object({
  name: z.string().trim().min(1, "Enter your full name"),
  postcode: z.string().regex(/^\d{4}$/, "Enter your postcode, which is 4 digits"),
});
const membership = z.object({
  interest: z.string().min(1, "Choose your main interest"),
  frequency: z.string().min(1, "Choose how often you will join night watches"),
});
type Details = z.infer<typeof details>;
type Membership = z.infer<typeof membership>;

const STEPS = ["Your details", "Membership details", "Check and submit"];

/** Step 1. Each step is its own form, validated before moving on. */
function DetailsStep({ initial, onNext }: { initial: Details; onNext: (v: Details) => void }) {
  const form = useAppForm({ defaultValues: initial, validators: { onChange: details }, onSubmit: ({ value }) => onNext(value) });
  return (
    <form.AppForm>
      <form.Form aria-label={STEPS[0]}>
        <form.ErrorSummary />
        <form.AppField name="name">{(field) => <field.TextField label="Full name" autoComplete="name" />}</form.AppField>
        <form.AppField name="postcode">{(field) => <field.TextField label="Postcode" inputMode="numeric" autoComplete="postal-code" helperText="4 digits, to match you with a chapter." />}</form.AppField>
        <div><form.SubmitButton>Continue</form.SubmitButton></div>
      </form.Form>
    </form.AppForm>
  );
}

/** Step 2. */
function MembershipStep({ initial, onNext, onBack }: { initial: Membership; onNext: (v: Membership) => void; onBack: () => void }) {
  const form = useAppForm({ defaultValues: initial, validators: { onChange: membership }, onSubmit: ({ value }) => onNext(value) });
  return (
    <form.AppForm>
      <form.Form aria-label={STEPS[1]}>
        <form.ErrorSummary />
        <form.AppField name="interest">
          {(field) => (
            <field.Select
              label="Main interest"
              placeholder="Choose one"
              options={[
                { value: "Night watches", label: "Night watches" },
                { value: "Tear-pool mapping", label: "Tear-pool mapping" },
                { value: "Folklore archive", label: "Folklore archive" },
              ]}
            />
          )}
        </form.AppField>
        <form.AppField name="frequency">
          {(field) => (
            <field.RadioGroup
              legend="How often will you join night watches?"
              options={[
                { value: "monthly", label: "Monthly" },
                { value: "two-monthly", label: "Every two months" },
                { value: "six-monthly", label: "Every six months" },
              ]}
            />
          )}
        </form.AppField>
        <div className={styles.actions}>
          <form.SubmitButton>Continue</form.SubmitButton>
          <Button variant="ghost" onClick={onBack}>Back</Button>
        </div>
      </form.Form>
    </form.AppForm>
  );
}

/**
 * A form in steps. Answers are kept as you go back and forth, the heading
 * takes focus on each new step so screen readers announce it, and nothing
 * is sent until the last step.
 */
export function MembershipApplicationSteps() {
  const [step, setStep] = React.useState(0);
  const [answers, setAnswers] = React.useState<{ details: Details; membership: Membership }>({
    details: { name: "", postcode: "" },
    membership: { interest: "", frequency: "" },
  });
  const [done, setDone] = React.useState(false);
  const headingRef = React.useRef<HTMLHeadingElement>(null);
  const first = React.useRef(true);
  React.useEffect(() => {
    if (first.current) { first.current = false; return; }
    headingRef.current?.focus();
  }, [step]);

  if (done) return <Alert tone="success" role="status">Application received for {answers.details.name}.</Alert>;

  return (
    <div className={styles.steps}>
      <div className={styles.stepHeader}>
        <Text tone="secondary">Step {step + 1} of {STEPS.length}</Text>
        <Heading level={2} ref={headingRef} tabIndex={-1}>{STEPS[step]}</Heading>
        <ProgressBar value={((step + 1) / STEPS.length) * 100} ariaLabel={`Step ${step + 1} of ${STEPS.length}`} />
      </div>
      {step === 0 ? (
        <DetailsStep initial={answers.details} onNext={(v) => { setAnswers((a) => ({ ...a, details: v })); setStep(1); }} />
      ) : step === 1 ? (
        <MembershipStep
          initial={answers.membership}
          onBack={() => setStep(0)}
          onNext={(v) => { setAnswers((a) => ({ ...a, membership: v })); setStep(2); }}
        />
      ) : (
        <>
          <dl className={styles.review}>
            <dt>Full name</dt><dd>{answers.details.name}</dd>
            <dt>Postcode</dt><dd>{answers.details.postcode}</dd>
            <dt>Main interest</dt><dd>{answers.membership.interest}</dd>
            <dt>Night watches</dt><dd>{answers.membership.frequency}</dd>
          </dl>
          <div className={styles.actions}>
            <Button variant="primary" onClick={() => setDone(true)}>Submit application</Button>
            <Button variant="ghost" onClick={() => setStep(1)}>Back</Button>
          </div>
        </>
      )}
    </div>
  );
}
```

# Server errors

Return the API's answer from `validators.onSubmitAsync`:

- `{ fields: { username: "That username is taken. Try another one." } }` puts a
  message on a field, as if the client had found it.
- `{ form: "We couldn't create your login just now…" }` is for problems that
  belong to no field. It shows in the error summary.
- Return `undefined` when everything is fine, and `onSubmit` runs.

The submit button is busy while the check runs, and a failed submit moves focus
to the summary as usual. Map your API's error codes to messages people can act
on; don't show the raw error.

```tsx
import React from "react";
import { z } from "zod";
import { Alert } from "@squonkstack/ui";
import { useAppForm } from "@squonkstack/ui/form";

const schema = z.object({
  username: z.string().trim().min(3, "Enter a username of at least 3 characters"),
  email: z.email("Enter an email address in the right format, like name@example.org"),
});

/** Stands in for the server: some answers only it can give. */
async function createLogin(value: z.infer<typeof schema>) {
  await new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, 400));
  if (value.username === "service-down") return { form: "We couldn't create your login just now. Try again in a few minutes." };
  const fields: Record<string, string> = {};
  if (value.username.toLowerCase() === "aroha") fields.username = "That username is taken. Try another one.";
  if (value.email.endsWith("@example.com")) fields.email = "Use a work email address, not a personal one";
  return Object.keys(fields).length ? { fields } : undefined;
}

/**
 * Errors from the server land where the client's own errors do: on the
 * field, and in the error summary. A form-level message covers what
 * belongs to no field.
 */
export function ServerErrorsForm() {
  const [created, setCreated] = React.useState<string>();
  const form = useAppForm({
    defaultValues: { username: "", email: "" },
    validators: { onChange: schema, onSubmitAsync: async ({ value }) => createLogin(value) },
    onSubmit: async ({ value }) => setCreated(`Created a login for ${value.username}.`),
  });

  return (
    <form.AppForm>
      <form.Form aria-label="Create a login">
        <form.ErrorSummary />
        <form.AppField name="username">
          {(field) => <field.TextField label="Username" autoComplete="username" helperText={'Try "aroha" (taken) or "service-down".'} />}
        </form.AppField>
        <form.AppField name="email">
          {(field) => <field.TextField label="Work email" type="email" helperText="Any address ending @example.com is refused." />}
        </form.AppField>
        <div><form.SubmitButton pendingLabel="Creating…">Create login</form.SubmitButton></div>
        {created ? <Alert tone="success" role="status">{created}</Alert> : null}
      </form.Form>
    </form.AppForm>
  );
}
```

# The basics

A form is a schema, a `useAppForm` call, and the fields inside `form.AppForm`:

1. **Describe the rules** with a Zod schema. Each rule carries the message people see.
2. **Create the form** with `useAppForm`: the starting values, the schema as the
   `onChange` validator, and what to do on submit.
3. **Render the fields** with `form.AppField`. Its `name` is type-checked
   against `defaultValues`, and `field.TextField`, `field.RadioGroup` and the rest
   take the same props as the components they wrap, minus value, change and error.
4. **Add `form.ErrorSummary` and `form.SubmitButton`** inside `form.Form`.

```tsx
import React from "react";
import { z } from "zod";
import { Alert } from "@squonkstack/ui";
import { useAppForm } from "@squonkstack/ui/form";

const schema = z.object({
  fullName: z.string().trim().min(1, "Enter your full name"),
  email: z.email("Enter an email address in the right format, like name@example.co.nz"),
  phone: z.string().regex(/^[\d\s+()-]*$/, "Enter a phone number using only numbers, spaces and +"),
  contactBy: z.string().min(1, "Choose how we should contact you"),
});

/** The basics: a schema, bound fields, an error summary and a submit button. */
export function ContactDetailsForm() {
  const [saved, setSaved] = React.useState<string>();
  const form = useAppForm({
    defaultValues: { fullName: "", email: "", phone: "", contactBy: "" },
    validators: { onChange: schema },
    onSubmit: async ({ value }) => setSaved(`Saved. We'll contact ${value.fullName} by ${value.contactBy}.`),
  });

  return (
    <form.AppForm>
      <form.Form aria-label="Contact details">
        <form.ErrorSummary />
        <form.AppField name="fullName">{(field) => <field.TextField label="Full name" autoComplete="name" />}</form.AppField>
        <form.AppField name="email">{(field) => <field.TextField label="Email" type="email" autoComplete="email" />}</form.AppField>
        <form.AppField name="phone">
          {(field) => <field.TextField label="Phone (optional)" type="tel" autoComplete="tel" helperText="For a call back about this account only." />}
        </form.AppField>
        <form.AppField name="contactBy">
          {(field) => (
            <field.RadioGroup
              legend="How should we contact you?"
              direction="row"
              options={[
                { value: "email", label: "Email" },
                { value: "phone", label: "Phone" },
                { value: "post", label: "Post" },
              ]}
            />
          )}
        </form.AppField>
        <div><form.SubmitButton>Save contact details</form.SubmitButton></div>
        {saved ? <Alert tone="success" role="status">{saved}</Alert> : null}
      </form.Form>
    </form.AppForm>
  );
}
```

## Bound fields

| In `form.AppField` | Wraps | Value |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `field.TextField` | [TextField](squonk-ui://components/text-field) | `string` |
| `field.Textarea` | [Textarea](squonk-ui://components/textarea) | `string` |
| `field.CurrencyField` | [CurrencyField](squonk-ui://components/currency-field) | `number \| null` (major units, `null` when empty) |
| `field.Select` | [Select](squonk-ui://components/select), including `searchable` | `string` (`""` for none) |
| `field.Checkbox` | [Checkbox](squonk-ui://components/checkbox) | `boolean` |
| `field.Switch` | [Switch](squonk-ui://components/switch) | `boolean` |
| `field.CheckboxGroup` | [CheckboxGroup] | `string[]` |
| `field.RadioGroup` | [RadioGroup] | `string` |

Anything else: see [Custom fields](squonk-ui://guides/patterns-forms-custom-fields).

## Validate on change, show on blur

`validators: { onChange: schema }` checks the whole form on every change, which
keeps errors current as people fix them. The fields still hold each error back
until the field is left or a submit is tried. Use `onBlur` instead only for rules
too slow to run on every keystroke.

# Validation

## Rules that compare fields

A confirmation that must match, or an end date after a start date, is a rule on
the whole object. Put it in `.refine` with a `path`, so the message lands on the
field it is about.

Add `when: () => true`. By default Zod skips object-level rules while any field
has an error, so someone who fixes one problem would discover the next only
after resubmitting. With it, every problem shows at once.

```tsx
import React from "react";
import { z } from "zod";
import { Alert } from "@squonkstack/ui";
import { useAppForm } from "@squonkstack/ui/form";

const ACCOUNT = /^\d{2}-\d{4}-\d{7}-\d{2,3}$/;

// Cross-field rules sit on the object. `when: () => true` runs them even
// while another field is invalid, so every problem shows at once; `path`
// puts the message on the field it is about.
const schema = z
  .object({
    accountName: z.string().trim().min(1, "Enter the name on the account"),
    accountNumber: z.string().regex(ACCOUNT, "Enter the account number in the format 12-3456-7890123-00"),
    confirmNumber: z.string().min(1, "Enter the account number again"),
    from: z.string().min(1, "Enter the date the change starts"),
    until: z.string(),
  })
  .refine((v) => v.confirmNumber === "" || v.confirmNumber === v.accountNumber, {
    message: "The account numbers don't match",
    path: ["confirmNumber"],
    when: () => true,
  })
  .refine((v) => !v.until || !v.from || v.until > v.from, {
    message: "The end date must be after the start date",
    path: ["until"],
    when: () => true,
  });

/** Rules that compare fields: a confirmation that must match and a date range. */
export function BankAccountForm() {
  const [saved, setSaved] = React.useState(false);
  const form = useAppForm({
    defaultValues: { accountName: "", accountNumber: "", confirmNumber: "", from: "", until: "" },
    validators: { onChange: schema },
    onSubmit: async () => setSaved(true),
  });

  return (
    <form.AppForm>
      <form.Form aria-label="Change credit payout account">
        <form.ErrorSummary />
        <form.AppField name="accountName">{(field) => <field.TextField label="Name on the account" />}</form.AppField>
        <form.AppField name="accountNumber">
          {(field) => <field.TextField label="Account number" inputMode="numeric" helperText="For example, 12-3456-7890123-00" />}
        </form.AppField>
        <form.AppField name="confirmNumber">
          {(field) => <field.TextField label="Confirm account number" inputMode="numeric" autoComplete="off" />}
        </form.AppField>
        <form.AppField name="from">{(field) => <field.TextField label="Use this account from" type="date" />}</form.AppField>
        <form.AppField name="until">
          {(field) => <field.TextField label="Until (optional)" type="date" helperText="Leave empty to keep using it." />}
        </form.AppField>
        <div><form.SubmitButton>Save bank account</form.SubmitButton></div>
        {saved ? <Alert tone="success" role="status">Credits will be paid into the new account.</Alert> : null}
      </form.Form>
    </form.AppForm>
  );
}
```

## Checks against the server

Some answers only the server knows: does this member exist, is this username
free? Put the check on the field with `onChangeAsync`:

- **Check the format first** and only call the server when it passes.
- **Debounce** with `onChangeAsyncDebounceMs`, so the check runs once typing stops.
- **Say it's checking.** `field.state.meta.isValidating` is true while the check
  runs; show it in the help text.

```tsx
import React from "react";
import { z } from "zod";
import { Alert } from "@squonkstack/ui";
import { useAppForm } from "@squonkstack/ui/form";

const KNOWN: Record<string, string> = { "SQ-104-772": "Aroha Ngata", "SQ-208-315": "Tui Harrow" };

/** Stands in for a request to the server. */
const lookUp = (id: string) =>
  new Promise<string | undefined>((resolve) => setTimeout(() => resolve(KNOWN[id]), 600));

const idFormat = z.string().regex(/^SQ-\d{3}-\d{3}$/, "Enter a membership number in the format SQ-104-772");

/**
 * A check that needs the server. The format rule runs on every change; the
 * lookup runs only when the format is right, half a second after typing
 * stops, and the field says it is checking meanwhile.
 */
export function MemberLookupForm() {
  const [member, setMember] = React.useState<string>();
  const form = useAppForm({
    defaultValues: { membershipNumber: "" },
    onSubmit: async ({ value }) => setMember(`${KNOWN[value.membershipNumber]} is now linked to your household membership.`),
  });

  return (
    <form.AppForm>
      <form.Form aria-label="Link a household member">
        <form.ErrorSummary />
        <form.AppField
          name="membershipNumber"
          validators={{
            onChange: idFormat,
            onChangeAsyncDebounceMs: 500,
            onChangeAsync: async ({ value }) =>
              idFormat.safeParse(value).success && !(await lookUp(value)) ? "We can't find a member with that number" : undefined,
          }}
        >
          {(field) => (
            <field.TextField
              label="Member's membership number"
              helperText={
                field.state.meta.isValidating
                  ? "Checking…"
                  : KNOWN[field.state.value] ?? "Try SQ-104-772, or any other number to see the not-found message."
              }
            />
          )}
        </form.AppField>
        <div><form.SubmitButton pendingLabel="Checking…">Link member</form.SubmitButton></div>
        {member ? <Alert tone="success" role="status">{member}</Alert> : null}
      </form.Form>
    </form.AppForm>
  );
}
```

## Choosing where a rule goes

| Rule | Put it in |
| --- | --- |
| About one field's own value | The schema, on that field |
| Comparing fields | `.refine` on the object, with `path` and `when: () => true` |
| Needs the server, worth checking as they type | `onChangeAsync` on the field |
| Needs the server, only on submit | `onSubmitAsync` on the form. See [Server errors](squonk-ui://guides/patterns-forms-server-errors) |

# Menus and commands

A [DropdownMenu](squonk-ui://components/dropdown-menu) holds a short list
of things to **do** with an item. It has no value of its own and closes after a
choice. Your app owns what each command does, and any confirmation after it.

**Example:** Open it with the keyboard (Enter or ↓), move with the arrows, choose with Enter, close with Escape: focus returns to the trigger.

```tsx
export function RowActionsExample() {
  const [done, setDone] = React.useState<string>();
  return (
    <VStack gap="sm">
      <Cluster gap="sm" align="center">
        <Text weight="bold">Dues statement 1042</Text>
        <Badge tone="warning">Due soon</Badge>
        <DropdownMenu
          triggerLabel="Actions for statement 1042"
          trigger={<MoreIcon size="control" />}
          items={[
            { key: "view", label: "View", onSelect: () => setDone("Opened statement 1042") },
            { key: "copy", label: "Duplicate", onSelect: () => setDone("Duplicated statement 1042") },
            { key: "send", label: "Send reminder", onSelect: () => setDone("Reminder sent") },
            "separator",
            { key: "void", label: "Void statement", tone: "danger", onSelect: () => setDone("Voided (in a real app, after a confirmation)") },
          ]}
        />
      </Cluster>
      <Text tone="secondary" role="status">{done ?? "Choose an action from the menu."}</Text>
    </VStack>
  );
}

/** A menu whose trigger says nothing about what it does. */
```

## The rules

- **Name the trigger for the item**: "Actions for statement 1042". An icon-only
  trigger still needs a name; `triggerLabel` gives it one.
- **Use verbs**: "Send reminder", not "Reminder".
- **Group related commands, and put destructive ones last**, after a separator,
  with `tone: "danger"`. Confirm or offer undo as on
  [Destructive actions](squonk-ui://guides/patterns-destructive-actions).
- **Keep it short.** A long searchable list is a [Select](squonk-ui://components/select)
  with `searchable`; places to go are [links](squonk-ui://guides/patterns-app-navigation).

**Do:** The trigger names the item it acts on, and the destructive command is last.

```tsx
export function RowActionsExample() {
  const [done, setDone] = React.useState<string>();
  return (
    <VStack gap="sm">
