# Switches, checkboxes and saving

A [Switch](squonk-ui://components/switch) turns something **on or off**.
A [Checkbox](squonk-ui://components/checkbox) **selects** something. The
difference is what the user expects to happen when they press it.

## When a change takes effect

**Our guidance** (the sources describe the controls but not this timing rule):

- Use a **Switch** when the change applies straight away: "Email notifications",
  "Dark mode". Show that it worked, or that it failed, right there. If saving
  fails, move the switch back and say why.
- Use a **Checkbox** when the choice is part of something the user submits later:
  "I agree to the terms", "Send me a copy", or rows to act on. Nothing happens
  until they press the form's button.
- Don't mix the two in one form. A switch inside a form that only saves on
  Submit makes people unsure whether anything changed.

**Do:** A switch saves at once, says so, and moves back if saving fails. Tick the box below it to see a failure.

```tsx
export function InstantSwitchExample() {
  const [on, setOn] = React.useState(true);
  const [status, setStatus] = React.useState("Saved");
  const [failNext, setFailNext] = React.useState(false);
  return (
    <VStack gap="sm">
      <Switch
        label="Email reminders"
        helperText="Before each due date. You'll still get notices."
        checked={on}
        onChange={(next) => {
          setOn(next);
          setStatus("Saving…");
          window.setTimeout(() => {
            if (failNext) {
              setOn(!next);
              setStatus("Couldn't save. Your reminders are unchanged.");
            } else setStatus("Saved");
          }, 700);
        }}
      />
      <Text tone="secondary" role="status">{status}</Text>
      <Checkbox label="Make the next save fail (to see the recovery)" checked={failNext} onChange={setFailNext} />
    </VStack>
  );
}

/** A checkbox that only counts when the form is submitted. */
```

**Do:** A checkbox in a form does nothing until the form is submitted.

```tsx
export function SubmittedCheckboxExample() {
  const [sent, setSent] = React.useState(false);
  return (
    <form onSubmit={(event) => { event.preventDefault(); setSent(true); }}>
      <VStack gap="sm">
        <Checkbox label="Email me a copy of this report" />
        <div><Button type="submit" variant="primary">Log sighting</Button></div>
        {sent ? <Text role="status">Logged.</Text> : null}
      </VStack>
    </form>
  );
}

// ---- Labels, help and validation ----------------------------------------------

/** A visible label, help that explains the format, and an instruction as the error. */
```

## Labels describe the setting, not the state

The label stays the same when the switch flips: "Email notifications", not
"Notifications are on". The switch itself shows the state. Use `helperText` for
consequences ("You'll still get security alerts").

## Errors

A setting that applies immediately reports failure where it happened, not as a
form error. A checkbox that must be ticked to continue gets an `error` like any
other form field.

